% ============================================================================
% hurricane-categories.query.adj — a worked recall over the meteorology
% hurricane-categories library.
% ============================================================================
% The shape a learner (or an LLM) produces to ANSWER "how much damage does a
% category 4 hurricane do?": it states no meteorology fact of its own — it only
% `import`s the grounded table and asks binding queries. The engine resolves
% each `?` against the `damage_level` rows and returns the descriptor + the
% table's source/locator/trust.
%
% The fifth query runs the relation BACKWARD (bind the descriptor
% `catastrophic_damage`, recall which categories carry it — HONESTLY category_4
% AND category_5, because the NHC source opens both paragraphs with the same
% "Catastrophic damage will occur" sentence). A key that is not one of the five
% Saffir-Simpson categories abstains honestly — the engine never invents a
% damage word.
%
% Run (from this directory so the relative import resolves):
%     ../../../../packages/rust/target/debug/adj-lang-cli hurricane-categories.query.adj
% ============================================================================

import "hurricane-categories.adj"

? damage_level(category_1, $Descriptor)          % expect some_damage
? damage_level(category_2, $Descriptor)          % expect extensive_damage
? damage_level(category_3, $Descriptor)          % expect devastating_damage
? damage_level(category_4, $Descriptor)          % expect catastrophic_damage
? damage_level(category_5, $Descriptor)          % expect catastrophic_damage
? damage_level($Category, catastrophic_damage)   % reverse bind — expect category_4 AND category_5
? damage_level(category_6, $Descriptor)          % expect abstain — there is no category 6 on the scale
